ially for track and field or swimming.", "tags": [], "examples": ["track meet", "swim meet"], "quotes": [{"text": "Everyone has to experience their first swim meet. They have to get through their first race, their first DQ (disqualification), and their first miss/scratch of an event. Like all swimmers, my first swim meet was nerve-wracking.", "reference": "2009, Alexandria Mangas, Janet Hommel Mangas, Oxygen for the Swimmer, Xulon Press, →ISBN, page 91:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(hunting) A gathering of riders, horses and hounds for foxhunting; a field meet for hunting.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(rail transport) A meeting of two trains in opposite directions on a single track, when one is put into a siding to let the other cross.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": ["pass"], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(informal) A meeting.", "tags": ["informal"], "examples": ["OK, let's arrange a meet with Tyler and ask him."], "quotes": [{"text": "You feel me? You use these phones to set up a meet, go to that meet… and talk face to face, period.", "reference": "2002, George Pelecanos, “Cleaning Up”, in The Wire, season 1, episode 12 (television production):"}, {"text": "So what do you wanna do? I wanna be absolutely fucking sure. That's what I wanna do. We arrange a meet. I'll feel him out a little bit.", "reference": "2004, Matthew Weiner, “Rat Pack”, in The Sopranos, season 5, episode 2 (television production):"}, {"text": "Rosen assured Cregger that he had left no paper trail in bringing the rock into the States. Pretending to be reassured, Cregger agreed to a location for a meet: Tuna’s, a small restaurant and margarita bar off West Dixie highway in North Miami Beach.", "reference": "2012 February 23, Joe Kloc, “The Case of the Missing Moon Rocks”, in Wired, →ISSN:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(algebra) The greatest lower bound, an operation between pairs of elements in a lattice, denoted by the symbol ∧.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": ["join"], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "adjective", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/miːt/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/mit/", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "meeter", "tags": ["comparative"]}, {"word": "meetest", "tags": ["superlative"]}, {"word": "mete", "tags": ["alternative", "obsolete"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(archaic) Suitable; right; proper.", "tags": ["archaic"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "It ſeemes not meete, nor wholeſome to my place, / To be producted, (as, if I ſtay, I ſhall,) / Againſt the Moore. […]", "reference": "c. 1603–1604 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Tragedie of Othello, the Moore of Venice”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act I, scene i], page 311:"}, {"text": "And the LORD God said, It is not good that the man should be alone: I will make him an helpe meet for him.", "reference": "1611, The Holy Bible, […] (King James Version), London: […] Robert Barker, […], →OCLC, Genesis 2:18:"}, {"text": "And Moses said, It is not meet so to do; for we shall sacrifice the abomination of the Egyptians to the Lord our God: lo, shall we sacrifice the abomination of the Egyptians before their eyes, and will they not stone us?", "reference": "1611, The Holy Bible, […] (King James Version), London: […] Robert Barker, […], →OCLC, Exodus 8:26:"}, {"text": "[…] it is therefore but meet, that in this place we set down who the Pequod's harpooneers were, and to what headsman each of them belonged.", "reference": "1851 November 14, Herman Melville, chapter XXVII, in Moby-Dick; or, The Whale, 1st American edition, New York, N.Y.: Harper & Brothers; London: Richard Bentley, →OCLC, page 131:"}, {"text": "The mountain birds are sweeter,\nBut the valley birds are fatter,\nAnd so we deemed it meeter\nTo carry off the latter.", "reference": "1958, T[erence] H[anbury] White, chapter VIII, in The Once and Future King, New York, N.Y.: G. P. Putnam's Sons, →ISBN, book I (The Sword in the Stone):"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) Submissive; passive.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/meet", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}